## Design Rules

Every subsystem inherits these. Project-specific additions at the end.

1. **Pure crates are pure.** `#![no_std]`, `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`, no heap, no
   hardware dependency. Build and test on host. All logic lives here.
2. **Hardware is behind traits.** Register access, DMA descriptors, anything with side
   effects is reached through a trait. Harnesses substitute a model device.
3. **Encoding is explicit.** Wire formats and register layouts are built with explicit
   byte and bit operations. No struct overlays on buffers, no `transmute`, no native
   endianness dependence. {{Target is big-endian; host is little-endian.}}
4. **Capacity is a parameter.** Fixed-size structures take capacity as a const generic
   or named const. Harnesses use small N; deployment uses the real N.
5. **Loops have stated bounds.** Derivable from a capacity const. The harness plan
   records the unwind limit.
6. **No panic paths on the hot path.** No `unwrap`, `expect`, or unchecked indexing in
   pure crates on {{the data path}}.
7. **State has one owner.** See Concurrency Model.
8. **`unsafe` cites its TA.** Every `unsafe` block's `// SAFETY:` comment names the TA
   ID it relies on.
9. {{Project-specific rule}}

